            <ArticleTitle>Flare-up incidence and related factors in Nigerian adults.</ArticleTitle>
            <Pagination>
                <MedlinePgn>120-3</MedlinePgn>
            </Pagination>
            <Abstract>
                <AbstractText Label="AIM" NlmCategory="OBJECTIVE">To determine the incidence of flare-up and the effect of age, gender, visit type, treatment duration, preoperative pain and intraoperative pain on flare-up in Nigerian adults.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="MATERIALS AND METHODS" NlmCategory="METHODS">A total of 175 participants, aged 18 to 60 years with a necrotic central incisor, with or without preoperative pain, participated. They received postoperative paracetamol tablets and were asked to report back if unbearable pain/swelling developed.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="RESULTS" NlmCategory="RESULTS">A 10% flare-up rate was recorded, while none of the studied factors had a significant relationship with flare-up.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="CONCLUSION" NlmCategory="CONCLUSIONS">The relationships between flare-up and studied related factors were not proven.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="CLINICAL SIGNIFICANCE" NlmCategory="CONCLUSIONS">Age, gender, visit type, treatment duration, preoperative pain and intraoperative pain have no effect on flare-up incidence.</AbstractText>
            </Abstract>
